or Logarithms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049879 #edb/ref 1033 1475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049879 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "1a5322f4-e5da-55f5-a61c-018264a8843c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050497 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050497 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050497 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050497 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050497 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050497 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050497 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050497 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050497 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050497 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050497 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050497 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050497 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050497 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050497 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050497 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050497 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving Quadratic Equations with No Constant Term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050497 #edb/ref 1033 393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050497 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "4dd99b27-25f2-551e-b641-cafc64c1d0b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115040 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4149" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115040 #edb/ref 1071 "If $\\log_2(x^2-6)=\\log_2(10),$ then what is the value of $x?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115040 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d833825d-4ac4-556d-95a1-bb328d5b5bb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115040 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}-6) & ={log}_{2}⁡(10) \\\\ {x}^{2}-6 & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=4$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}-6) & ={log}_{2}⁡({4}^{2}-6) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(16-6) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(10).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=4$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-4$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}-6) & ={log}_{2}⁡((-4)^{2}-6) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(16-6) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(10).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-4$ is a valid solution.\n\nSo, we conclude that the solutions are $x = \\pm 4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115042 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4150" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115042 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\ln (2x^2)=\\ln (3x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115042 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "088cff27-4c06-542c-876b-d3d2ed7b0504" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115042 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =3x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3x & =0 \\\\ x(2x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=0$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2(0)^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3(0)) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not valid because $\\ln \\left(0\\right)$ is undefined. Therefore, $x=0$ is not a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3(\\frac{3}{2})) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{2}).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $x =\\dfrac{3}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115043 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4151" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115043 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve $\\log (x-1)=\\log (2x+1)$ for $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115043 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d164cff6-3d50-57c8-a118-8681aaf0c1f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115043 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-1) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+1) \\\\ x-1 & =2x+1 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-1) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-2-1) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2(-2)+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-3) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\log (-3)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115213 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115213 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115213 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115213 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115618 #edb/ref