ef 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546959 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation ${log}_{6}⁡({x}^{2}+20)={log}_{6}⁡(-10x-5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546959 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0107835e-4e3e-5394-afc5-c0c0506cc080" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546959 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{6}⁡({x}^{2}+20) & ={log}_{6}⁡(-10x-5) \\\\ {x}^{2}+20 & =-10x-5 \\\\ {x}^{2}+10x+25 & =0 \\\\ (x+5)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=-5$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{6}⁡({x}^{2}+20) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{6}⁡(-10x-5) \\\\ {log}_{6}⁡((-5)^{2}+20) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{6}⁡(-10(-5)-5) \\\\ {log}_{6}⁡(45) & ={log}_{6}⁡(45).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-5$ is a valid solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546998 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546998 #edb/ref 1069 "q-100763" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546998 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546998 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve $\\operatorname{log}⁡(4x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(5x+9)$ for $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546998 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "454648d5-f41c-5a23-83bf-f9148d806a00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546998 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(4x) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(5x+9) \\\\ 4x & =5x+9 \\\\ x & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-9$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(4x) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(5x+9) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(4(-9)) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(5(-9)+9) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-36) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(-36)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\operatorname{log}⁡(-36)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547014 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547014 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110796" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547014 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547014 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${log}_{5}⁡(4-x)=2{log}_{5}⁡2,$ then what is the value of $x?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547014 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6dbcbc9c-b30e-515a-9390-1cde473a0411" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547014 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(4-x) & =2{log}_{5}⁡2 \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(4-x) & ={log}_{5}⁡{2}^{2} \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(4-x) & ={log}_{5}⁡4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(4-x) & ={log}_{5}⁡4 \\\\ 4-x & =4 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=0$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(4-x) & ={log}_{5}⁡(4-0) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡(4) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡({2}^{2}) \\\\  & =2{log}_{5}⁡2.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ is a valid solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547058 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547058 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79866" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547058 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547058 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation ${log}_{2}⁡(2x-5)=\\frac{1}{3}{log}_{2}⁡(27).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547058 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d3ac80cd-6459-5be8-953c-5d7b28bc8da8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547058 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(2x-5) & =\\frac{1}{3}{log}_{2}⁡(27) \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(2x-5) & ={log}_{2}⁡({27}^{1/3}) \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(2x-5) & ={log}_{2}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(2x-5) & ={log}_{2}⁡(3) \\\\ 2x-5 & =3 \\\\ 2x & =8 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=4$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(2x-5) & ={log}_{2}⁡(2(4)-5) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{log}_{2}⁡(27).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=4$ is a valid solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547065 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547065 #edb/ref 1069 "q-100769" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547065 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547065 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x-5)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(7-x),$ what is the value of $x?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547065 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "de567b40-8a36-58e6-967e-d32432dbd495" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547065 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x-5) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(7-x) \\\\ 3x-5 & =7-x \\\\ 4x & =12 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=3$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(3(3)-5) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(7-3) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=3$ is a valid solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
